+scientific_hypotheses:
+  - id: PM4-H1
+    title: Plant-Substrate Variety and Microbial Processing Capacity
+    status: Unestablished
+    proposition: >-
+      At matched total fermentable-substrate intake, a defined increase in plant-substrate variety increases
+      the breadth of microbial processing of a prespecified glycan panel in the tested population. Changes in
+      functional community composition, including but not necessarily greater microbial richness, may mediate
+      this response.
+    proposed_direction: Plant-substrate variety → microbial substrate-processing breadth
+    rationale: >-
+      Defined-community experiments support substrate- and organism-specific processing, while human dietary
+      studies motivate testing plant-substrate variety. Community richness is a candidate mediator, not a
+      validated surrogate for processing capacity.
+    finding_links:
+      supporting: []
+      contradictory_or_null: []
+      contextual:
+        - pm_id: BRS5-FM2-PM4
+          finding_id: PM4-F1
+          href: '#pm4-f1'
+          explanation: Provides bounded pathway context; does not establish the complete hypothesis.
+        - pm_id: BRS5-FM2-PM4
+          finding_id: PM4-F2
+          href: '#pm4-f2'
+          explanation: Provides bounded pathway context; does not establish the complete hypothesis.
+    evidence_sources:
+      - study_id: patnode-2019
+        label: Patnode et al. (2019)
+        url: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6760872/
+        role: contextual
+        proposition: Defined community membership, competition and glycan exposure influence processing.
+        limitations: Gnotobiotic mouse experiments; not a general causal plant-variety or richness benefit in humans.
+      - study_id: mcdonald-2018
+        label: McDonald et al. (2018)
+        url: https://journals.asm.org/doi/full/10.1128/msystems.00031-18
+        role: contextual
+        proposition: Plant-variety categories were associated with microbiome measures.
+        limitations: Observational self-reported diet; no causal processing-flux or optimal threshold result.
+      - study_id: wastyk-2021
+        label: Wastyk et al. (2021)
+        url: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9020749/
+        role: contextual
+        proposition: >-
+          Human fibre and fermented-food interventions separate functional potential, diversity and immune
+          outcomes.
+        limitations: Gene abundance is not utilisation flux; no demonstrated richness mediation.
+      - study_id: stanford-2025
+        label: Stanford et al. (2025)
+        url: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12097182/
+        role: contextual
+        proposition: A CKD crossover tested higher versus lower plant variety.
+        limitations: Population-specific and composition-confounded; no consistent overall uremic-toxin reduction.
+      - study_id: creedon-2026
+        label: Creedon et al. (2026)
+        url: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42423005/
+        role: contextual
+        proposition: >-
+          A mixed-plant product changed composition and some symptoms, without significant between-group
+          microbiome health-rank differences.
+        limitations: >-
+          Product-specific; no demonstrated processing breadth or plant-count effect. Full-text richness
+          detail was not independently reverified.
+    limitations:
+      - >-
+        Plant count does not specify substrate chemistry, portions or preparation; matching fibre grams does
+        not match chemistry.
+      - >-
+        CAZyme abundance, stool metabolite concentrations and microbial richness do not measure utilisation
+        flux.
+      - >-
+        Greater richness need not improve processing; functional replacement, redundancy and competition may
+        alter outcomes.
+      - >-
+        No universal 30-plants threshold, barrier effect, cognitive benefit or clinical efficacy is
+        established.
+      - Studies of different diets and populations do not establish the complete causal chain.
+    alternative_explanations:
+      - Specific substrate chemistry or degrader abundance rather than richness may explain processing.
+      - Transit, microbial biomass, adherence and food preparation may explain apparent differences.
+    required_evidence:
+      - >-
+        Compare characterised dietary patterns at matched fermentable-substrate intake and energy, measuring a
+        prespecified glycan-utilisation panel.
+      - >-
+        Measure community composition, enzyme activity and actual processing separately; distinguish ex-vivo
+        capacity from in-vivo flux.
+      - >-
+        A precise processing null despite verified exposure counts against the intervention proposition;
+        processing gains without richness gains count against richness mediation, not necessarily the
+        intervention.
+    provenance:
+      - >-
+        Author-approved bounded hypothesis following the ten-candidate appraisal and individual review on 14
+        November 2026.
+      - >-
+        Approval registers the proposition only. No dietary admissions, SEC, publication gates, food mappings,
+        rankings or accepted graph edges change.
+    review_date: '2026-11-14'
